              "youtube_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=90auAVyZswc",
              "review_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=90auAVyZswc&t=1111s",
              "start_seconds": 1111,
              "end_seconds": 1200,
              "label": "Why deployment success still needs a live-service smoke check",
              "summary": "A successful deployment operation does not prove that the application works. Files can be copied and the application started, yet a configuration error or broken database connection can prevent useful behavior. Smoke checks test basic functionality immediately after deployment. Examples include reaching the home page with an HTTP 200 response, signing in with test credentials, and reaching a health endpoint. These checks are deliberately simple and fast—roughly one or two minutes—not comprehensive application testing.",
              "fact_ids": [
                "f1"
              ],
              "transcript_ref": "90auAVyZswc:1111-1200"
            }
          ],
          "group_title": "Deployment completion versus live-service validation",
          "bridge": {
            "before": "Watch for the distinction between completing deployment operations and confirming live application behavior. The endpoint examples illustrate quick smoke checks, not comprehensive testing.",
            "after": "The clip explains why smoke checks matter, but omits supplied-action execution, deployed-revision verification, and Lambda job-result reporting. The text covers those steps, including blocking progression on failure."
          },
          "default_mode": "video"
        }
      ]
